---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import GuideData from "../../components/GuideData.astro";
import EveryCut from "../../components/EveryCut.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import { bySlug, kicker } from "../../dossiers/registry.js";
import { dateStamp, bundleOf } from "../../dossiers/display.js";
const D = bySlug["external"];
// the periods this page names, read from the bundles at build time (never typed)
const MS = ["Jan", "Feb", "Mar", "Apr", "May", "Jun", "Jul", "Aug", "Sep", "Oct", "Nov", "Dec"];
const MF = ["January", "February", "March", "April", "May", "June", "July", "August", "September", "October", "November", "December"];
const last = (slug) => bundleOf(slug).dates.at(-1);
const qOf = (iso) => { const m = +iso.slice(5, 7) - 1; return `${MS[m]} to ${MS[m + 2]} ${iso.slice(0, 4)}`; };
const monOf = (iso) => `${MF[+iso.slice(5, 7) - 1]} ${iso.slice(0, 4)}`;
const dayOf = (iso) => `${+iso.slice(8, 10)} ${MF[+iso.slice(5, 7) - 1]} ${iso.slice(0, 4)}`;
const fyOf = (iso) => { const y = +iso.slice(0, 4); return `${y}-${String((y + 1) % 100).padStart(2, "0")}`; };
const Q = { bop: qOf(last("external-bop")), ppl: qOf(last("external-invisibles-people")), iip: qOf(last("external-iip-head")), sect: qOf(last("external-debt-sector-inr")) };
const M = { rupee: monOf(last("external-rupee")), reer: monOf(last("external-reer-2015")), int: monOf(last("external-intervention")), lrs: monOf(last("external-lrs")), fdi: monOf(last("external-fdi")), fpi: monOf(last("external-fpi")), resm: monOf(last("external-reserves-monthly")) };
const WK = dayOf(last("external-reserves-weekly"));
const FY = { ind: fyOf(last("external-bop-indicators")), ann: fyOf(last("external-bop-annual-head")) };
const DEBT = `March ${last("external-debt-annual").slice(0, 4)}`;
---
<Base
  title="The External Account · India Atlas"
  description={D.description}
  nav="guides"
  dateStamp={dateStamp(D)}
  crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / ${D.section} / <b>${D.crumbTitle}</b>`}
  crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`}
  canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/external/"
  ogImage={`/og/${D.slug}.png`}
>
<div class="page">

  <div class="titleblock break">
    <div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div>
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <h1 set:html={D.headline} />
      <p class="lede">Every rupee India earns from the world and every rupee it pays out is written down twice, once for what crossed the border and once for how it was paid. This guide reads that ledger: what the country sells and buys, who sends money in and who takes it out, what is kept in reserve, and what is owed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>Source <b>Reserve Bank of India, DBIE</b></span>
      <span>Balance of payments <b>Quarterly, to <span id="m-latest">–</span></b></span>
      <span>Reserves <b>Weekly, to <span id="m-week">–</span></b></span>
      <span>Rupee, flows <b>Monthly</b></span>
      <span>Units <b>US$ billion unless named</b></span>
      <span>Presentation <b>RBI's overall balance of payments</b></span>
    </div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Current account, the latest quarter</div><div class="v acc" id="n-ca">–</div><div class="d" id="d-ca">–</div><div class="nspark" id="nspark-ca"></div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Foreign exchange reserves, latest week</div><div class="v" id="n-res">–</div><div class="d" id="d-res">–</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Rupees per US dollar, monthly average</div><div class="v" id="n-inr">–</div><div class="d" id="d-inr">–</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Real effective exchange rate, 40 currencies</div><div class="v" id="n-reer">–</div><div class="d" id="d-reer">–</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- § 01 current reading: data inputs external-bop (all net lines), external-bop-gross, external-reserves-weekly, external-reer-2015; prose: generated slots -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The current reading</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit" id="u-reading">–</span></div>
    <div class="reading-top">
      <div><span class="rk">Current account</span><div class="big" id="rb-ca">–</div></div>
      <p class="rt-cap" id="rb-cap">–</p>
      <div class="rt-side"><span class="rk">Reserves, latest week</span><div class="rt-side-v" id="rb-res">–</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="reading-cols">
      <div class="reading-col">
        <span class="mk">What the quarter says</span>
        <Prose slot="external.current-reading.what-the-print-says" />
      </div>
      <div class="reading-col now">
        <span class="mk">What it does not say</span>
        <Prose slot="external.current-reading.what-it-does-not-say" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 living within its means: external-bop current_account, external-bop-indicators cad_to_gdp; evergreen + governed As of -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>Is India living within its means?</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">The current account, quarterly and as a share of GDP</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-ca" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Most quarters India pays the world more than it earns from it</h3>
            <div class="sub">Current account balance, US$ billion a quarter, fiscal quarters since April 2010. Below zero is a deficit.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-ca">–</div><div class="c">{Q.bop}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-ca"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, India's overall balance of payments (DBIE report 56), net, latest revision of each quarter.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The current account adds up everything India earns from the world in a quarter (exports of goods and services, income on Indian money invested abroad, money sent home) and takes away everything it pays out. A deficit means the country spent more abroad than it earned, and paid the difference with foreign money that came in: investment, loans, or deposits by Indians living abroad.</p>
            <p>A deficit is not a loss, any more than a family taking a home loan is losing money. What matters is its size against the economy, and whether the money that fills it stays.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {Q.bop}</span>
            <p>The latest quarter, <span id="ex-ca-q">–</span>, shows a balance of <b id="ex-ca-latest">–</b>. The deepest deficit in this run was <b id="ex-ca-trough">–</b>. Only <b id="ex-ca-surplus">–</b> quarters since 2010 ended in surplus: <span id="ex-ca-surplus-why">–</span>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-cagdp" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Measured against the economy, today's deficit is small</h3>
            <div class="sub">Current account balance as a share of GDP, fiscal years since 1970-71. The dotted line marks a deficit of 3% of GDP.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-cagdp">–</div><div class="c">{FY.ind}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-cagdp"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, balance of payments indicators (DBIE report 427), as published.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Dividing by GDP lets one year be compared with another fifty years apart. A deficit beyond about 3% of GDP is often treated as hard to finance for long; it is a rule of thumb, not a law. The years that crossed it were years of trouble: the run-up to the 1991 payments crisis, and 2011-12 and 2012-13, when the rupee fell sharply.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {FY.ind}</span>
            <p>The ratio for <span id="ex-cagdp-fy">–</span> is <b id="ex-cagdp">–</b>. The worst year on record is <b id="ex-cagdp-trough">–</b>; the deficit has gone beyond 3% of GDP in <b id="ex-cagdp-n3">–</b> fiscal years since 1970-71.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 how to read this: evergreen, human-written -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>What this measures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The balance of payments is double-entry bookkeeping for a country. Every transaction between someone in India and someone abroad is recorded twice. When a software firm in Bengaluru bills a bank in London, the export enters the current account and the dollars it earns enter the financial side. Because every entry has a matching entry, the whole account always balances; what people call a "deficit" is the balance of one part of it.</p>
      <p>The <b>current account</b> covers goods, services (software, travel, transport, business services), income (interest, dividends, wages earned across borders) and transfers (mostly money sent home by Indians working abroad). The <b>capital account</b>, in RBI's presentation, covers money that comes to stay or to be repaid: foreign direct investment, portfolio investment in shares and bonds, loans, banks' foreign borrowing, and deposits by non-resident Indians. What is left over moves the <b>reserves</b>.</p>
      <p>The Reserve Bank publishes the account every quarter, about twelve weeks after the quarter ends, first as a provisional estimate and then revised. It keeps two presentations. This guide draws RBI's own long-running layout, which its press release leads with and which runs back to 1990 (and, year by year, to 1950). The international standard layout (the IMF's sixth manual, BPM6), which RBI has published since 2011-12, files some lines differently; it is downloadable below and is never mixed with the other on one chart.</p>
      <p>Weekly reserves, the rupee's daily price, and monthly figures for foreign investment, borrowing and remittances fill in between the quarters. They come from the same source, the RBI's Database on Indian Economy.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 what fills the gap: external-bop goods/services/private_transfers/income/software; external-bop-annual-head -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>What pays for the goods</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">The parts of the current account</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-parts" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Services and money from Indians abroad pay for most of the goods India buys</h3>
            <div class="sub">Net goods, services, remittances and income, US$ billion a quarter, since April 2011.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-parts">–</div><div class="c">goods, {Q.bop}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-parts"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, overall balance of payments (DBIE report 56). Remittances are private transfers, net. Goods are on a balance-of-payments basis, not the monthly customs figures.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>India buys far more goods than it sells: crude oil, gold, electronics, machinery. That gap is the blue line, always below zero. Two flows climb against it. Services, led by software and business services, earn a growing surplus. Remittances, the money Indians working in the Gulf, America and elsewhere send to their families, add almost as much again. Income runs the other way: profits and interest paid to foreigners who invested in India exceed what Indian money earns abroad.</p>
            <p>When the phone in your pocket, the petrol in your scooter and the gold at a wedding are paid for, it is largely a nurse in Dubai and a coder in Pune who pay.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {Q.bop}</span>
            <p>Goods <b id="ex-p-goods">–</b>; services <b id="ex-p-svc">–</b>, of which software <b id="ex-p-soft">–</b>; remittances <b id="ex-p-rem">–</b>; income <b id="ex-p-inc">–</b>. Services and remittances together covered <b id="ex-p-cover">–</b> of the goods deficit.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-long" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Seventy-five years of the account: the goods gap opened, and invisibles grew to meet it</h3>
            <div class="sub">Net current account, goods and invisibles (services, transfers and income), US$ billion, fiscal years since 1950-51.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-long">–</div><div class="c">current account, {FY.ann}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-long"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, key components of India's balance of payments (DBIE report 875), net, as published.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>For four decades the numbers are small enough to look flat at this scale; the economy was closed and the rupee controlled. The lines fan out after the 1991 reforms. The goods deficit widens as Indians buy more of the world's oil, gold and electronics, and invisibles, mostly software and remittances, rise almost as fast. The current account, their sum, stays close to zero because the two move together.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {FY.ann}</span>
            <p>In 1990-91, the year that ended in the 1991 payments crisis, the current account was <b id="ex-long-91">–</b>. Net invisibles first passed US$ 10 billion in <b id="ex-long-inv10">–</b>. The latest full year in this table is <span id="ex-long-fy">–</span>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 05 money home and money out: external-bop-gross remittances_in; external-invisibles-people; external-lrs -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">05</span><h2>Money sent home, money sent out</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Remittances and the Liberalised Remittance Scheme</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-remit" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>What Indians abroad send home has grown every decade</h3>
            <div class="sub">Private transfers received, US$ billion a quarter, since April 1990. Gross receipts, before the much smaller sums sent out.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-remit">–</div><div class="c">{Q.bop}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-remit"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, overall balance of payments (DBIE report 56), private transfers, credit.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>This is money that arrives without anything being sold for it: wages earned by Indians abroad and sent to their families, gifts, donations to temples and charities, and money NRIs draw out of their Indian bank accounts to spend here. For a household in Kerala, Punjab or Bihar it pays for school fees, a house, a wedding. For the country it is the steadiest inflow there is: it rarely falls even when markets panic.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {Q.bop}</span>
            <p>Receipts were <b id="ex-r-q">–</b> in the latest quarter and <b id="ex-r-year">–</b> over the last four quarters.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-remitmix" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Most of it is wages sent home for the family</h3>
            <div class="sub">Private transfers received, by kind, US$ billion, {Q.ppl}, the latest quarter of RBI's detailed table.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-remitmix">–</div><div class="c">sent home for the family</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-remitmix" style="position:relative"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, invisibles by category of transactions (DBIE report 1076), receipts. The detailed table ends a year before the headline account.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The headline remittance figure mixes two different things. Money sent home by a worker abroad is income for a family. Money an NRI withdraws in India from a deposit already held here is a transfer of savings. Both count as private transfers. The bar on top is the first kind.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {Q.ppl}</span>
            <p>In <span id="ex-rm-q">–</span>, money sent home for families came to <b id="ex-rm-family">–</b> and withdrawals from NRI deposits to <b id="ex-rm-nri">–</b>. Indians working on foreign contracts earned another <b id="ex-rm-wages">–</b> in wages. Going the other way, private transfers paid abroad were <b id="ex-rm-out">–</b>, and Indians spent <b id="ex-rm-edu">–</b> on travel for education abroad.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-lrs" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Indians now send tens of billions of dollars abroad a year, mostly for travel</h3>
            <div class="sub">Outward remittances by resident individuals under the Liberalised Remittance Scheme, by purpose, US$ billion, fiscal years (each the sum of RBI's twelve monthly figures).</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-lrs">–</div><div class="c">latest full year</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-lrs"></div>
          <div class="plotzone" data-x="time"><div id="plot-lrs" style="position:relative"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, outward remittances under the LRS (DBIE report 793), monthly, summed by India Atlas into fiscal years; only complete years are drawn.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Any resident Indian may send up to US$ 250,000 a year abroad under the scheme: for a holiday, a child's college fees, support for a relative, shares in a foreign company. Travel here includes tour packages and spending on international cards. Much of it now carries tax collected at source, which is one reason the scheme's figures are watched closely.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {M.lrs}</span>
            <p>In <span id="ex-l-fy">–</span>, Indians sent <b id="ex-l-total">–</b> abroad under the scheme; travel made up <b id="ex-l-travel">–</b> of it and studies abroad <b id="ex-l-study">–</b>. In <span id="ex-l-edum">–</span>, travel for education alone took <b id="ex-l-edu">–</b>; the latest month published is <span id="ex-l-month">–</span>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 06 foreign money: external-bop fdi/portfolio; external-fdi; external-fpi -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">06</span><h2>Who brings money in, and how long it stays</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Direct and portfolio investment</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-flows" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Direct investment is steady; portfolio money comes and goes</h3>
            <div class="sub">Net foreign direct investment and net portfolio investment, US$ billion a quarter, since April 2010.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-flows">–</div><div class="c">portfolio, {Q.bop}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-flows"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, overall balance of payments (DBIE report 56). Net direct investment is investment into India less Indian investment abroad.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Direct investment buys a lasting stake: a factory, a share of a company large enough to have a say in it. It is hard to pull out quickly. Portfolio investment buys shares and bonds that can be sold in a day, and it leaves when interest rates rise abroad or markets turn nervous. A deficit financed by portfolio money is a deficit financed by money that may not stay.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {Q.bop}</span>
            <p>Net direct investment was <b id="ex-f-fdi">–</b> and net portfolio investment <b id="ex-f-fpi">–</b> in the latest quarter. Over the quarters drawn, portfolio flows typically land about <b id="ex-f-sdfpi">–</b> away from their own average in a quarter, against <b id="ex-f-sdfdi">–</b> for direct investment (one standard deviation); portfolio money left on net in <b id="ex-f-negfpi">–</b> quarters, direct investment in <b id="ex-f-negfdi">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-fdi" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Much of the direct investment that comes in also goes back out</h3>
            <div class="sub">Gross foreign direct investment into India, repatriation by foreign investors, and Indian companies' investment abroad, US$ billion, fiscal years (each the sum of RBI's monthly figures).</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-fdi">–</div><div class="c">gross inflows, latest full year</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-fdi"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, foreign investment inflows (DBIE report 34), monthly, summed by India Atlas into fiscal years; complete years only.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Headlines about "record FDI" usually quote the gross inflow. But private equity funds that bought into Indian companies sell out and take their money home, and Indian companies buy businesses abroad. What is left for the balance of payments is much smaller than the gross figure.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {M.fdi}</span>
            <p>In <span id="ex-fdi-fy">–</span> gross inflows were <b id="ex-fdi-gross">–</b>, repatriation <b id="ex-fdi-rep">–</b> (<b id="ex-fdi-share">–</b> of what came in), and Indian investment abroad <b id="ex-fdi-out">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-fpi" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Foreign investors in Indian shares and bonds change their minds month to month</h3>
            <div class="sub">Net purchases by foreign portfolio investors in the Indian market, ₹ thousand crore a month, since January 2016.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-fpi">–</div><div class="c">{M.fpi}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-fpi"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, net investment by FPIs in the Indian capital market (DBIE report 136), ₹ crore, from depository data. Not the same timing or coverage as the balance-of-payments portfolio line.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Each bar is a month of buying (above zero) or selling (below). When foreign funds sell, the stock market usually falls and the rupee weakens as they convert their rupees back into dollars. This is the money most likely to leave when the world gets nervous.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {M.fpi}</span>
            <p>The latest month is <span id="ex-fpi-m">–</span>. Foreign portfolio investors sold more than they bought in <b id="ex-fpi-out">–</b> months since 2016; the largest month of selling on record was <b id="ex-fpi-trough">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
