  </section>

  <!-- § 07 reserves: external-reserves-weekly, external-reserves-monthly, external-intervention; indicators import cover -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">07</span><h2>The reserves, and why they moved this week</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Weekly, US$ billion</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-res" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Twenty-five years of building a buffer</h3>
            <div class="sub">Foreign exchange reserves, US$ billion, every week since April 2001: the total, foreign currency assets and gold.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-res">–</div><div class="c">{WK}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-res"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, foreign exchange reserves, weekly (DBIE report 574, Weekly Statistical Supplement table 2). SDRs and the IMF reserve position make up the small rest.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Reserves are dollars, euros, yen, gold and claims on the IMF held by the RBI. They exist for bad days: to pay for imports if foreign money stops coming, and to stop the rupee falling too fast. Each week's figure moves for two reasons. The RBI buys or sells dollars in the market. And everything it holds that is not a dollar (gold, euros, yen, the bonds themselves) is valued in dollars at that week's prices, so a fall in the euro or in gold shrinks the pile without the RBI selling anything.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {WK}</span>
            <p>Reserves stood at <b id="ex-res-total">–</b> on <span id="ex-res-week">–</span>, a change of <b id="ex-res-wk">–</b> on the week before, against a peak of <b id="ex-res-peak">–</b>. Gold is <b id="ex-res-gold">–</b> of the total. At the end of <span id="ex-res-cover-fy">–</span> the reserves covered <b id="ex-res-cover">–</b> months of imports.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-resmove" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Reserves do not move only when the RBI buys or sells</h3>
            <div class="sub">Month-on-month change in total reserves (bars) against the RBI's net purchases (+) or sales (−) of dollars in the same month (dots), US$ billion, since August 2023.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-resmove">–</div><div class="c">change, {M.resm}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-resmove"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, reserves at month end (DBIE report 184) and sale and purchase of US dollars by the RBI (DBIE report 730). The month's change is computed by India Atlas from the two month-end figures.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Where a dot sits on its bar, the month's change was the RBI's own trading. Where they part, something else moved the pile: gold and other currencies revalued against the dollar, interest earned on the bonds held, forward contracts maturing. The RBI's purchase figures are published a few weeks after the reserves, so the latest months have bars but no dots.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {M.resm}</span>
            <p>Reserves changed by <b id="ex-mv-chg">–</b> in <span id="ex-mv-month">–</span>. In <span id="ex-mv-netm">–</span>, the latest month with both figures, the RBI's net purchases were <b id="ex-mv-net">–</b>. Over the months drawn, the two have differed by as much as <b id="ex-mv-gapmax">–</b> in a single month.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 08 the rupee: external-rupee usd; external-reer-2015 -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">08</span><h2>The rupee, against the dollar and against everyone</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Monthly</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-rupee" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>A dollar costs about three times as many rupees as in 1993</h3>
            <div class="sub">Rupees per US dollar, monthly average of the reference rate, since April 1993, the first full month after the rupee was left to the market.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-rupee">–</div><div class="c">{M.rupee}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-rupee"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, monthly average exchange rates (DBIE report 719). The table runs from 1966; for March 1992 to February 1993 (the dual exchange rate) it carries the official LERMS rate (₹25.89 to ₹26.20), while the market rate (₹29.46 to ₹32.65) is in DBIE report 1374, both downloadable below. The table has no figure for May 2024, so the line breaks there.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>A higher number is a weaker rupee. Over long periods the rupee loses ground because prices in India rise faster than in America: if Indian prices rise 5% a year and American prices 2%, a rupee that did not weaken would make Indian goods dearer abroad every year. The steps in the line are the moments when that slow pressure met a sudden one, such as 2008, 2013, 2018 and 2022.</p>
            <p>For a family, a weaker rupee means dearer petrol, phones, foreign tuition and holidays; for an exporter, or a family receiving money from abroad, more rupees for every dollar earned.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {M.rupee}</span>
            <p>A dollar cost <b id="ex-ru-v">–</b> on average in <span id="ex-ru-m">–</span>, against <b id="ex-ru-y">–</b> a year earlier: <b id="ex-ru-pct">–</b> more rupees for a dollar than a year before.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-reer" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>After prices, the rupee has weakened far less than the dollar rate says</h3>
            <div class="sub">Real and nominal effective exchange rate of the rupee, 40-currency trade-weighted basket, index 2015-16 = 100, monthly since April 2004. A rise is a stronger rupee.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-reer">–</div><div class="c">REER, {M.reer}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-reer"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, indices of REER and NEER (DBIE report 584), base 2015-16 = 100. Earlier bases (1985, 1993-94, 2004-05) and the 6-currency index are separate rulers, downloadable below, never joined to this one.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>India does not trade only with America. The nominal index (NEER) weighs the rupee against 40 currencies by how much India trades with each country. The real index (REER) then adjusts for prices: if the rupee falls 5% but Indian prices rise 5% more than partners' prices, Indian goods are no cheaper abroad, and the real rate has not moved. That is why the real rate, not the dollar rate, tells you whether Indian goods are getting more or less competitive.</p>
            <p>Read the gap between the two lines: the nominal index falls steadily because Indian inflation runs higher than its partners'; the real index falls much less because that fall roughly offsets the price gap.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {M.reer}</span>
            <p>In <span id="ex-re-m">–</span> the real index was <b id="ex-re-reer">–</b> and the nominal index <b id="ex-re-neer">–</b>, a gap of <b id="ex-re-gap">–</b> points; a year earlier the real index stood at <b id="ex-re-reery">–</b>. Below 100 means that, after prices, the rupee is cheaper than it was in 2015-16.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 09 the RBI's dollar desk: external-intervention, external-forward-book -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">09</span><h2>The RBI's dollar desk</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Monthly, US$ billion</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-desk" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The RBI sells dollars to slow the rupee's fall, and promises more for later</h3>
            <div class="sub">The RBI's net purchases (+) or sales (−) of US dollars each month (bars), and its outstanding forward position at the end of each month (line), US$ billion, since April 2015.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-desk">–</div><div class="c">forward book, {M.int}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-desk"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, sale and purchase of US dollars by the RBI (DBIE report 730). Net purchases, gross purchases and sales and the forward book as published; the forward book is a stock at the end of the month. In March 2014 the table repeats the purchase figure as the sale, so read the net for that month.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The rupee floats, but not freely. When it falls too fast, the RBI sells dollars from the reserves; when money floods in, it buys. A forward sale is a promise to deliver dollars at a future date: it supports the rupee today without spending reserves today, but the bill falls due. A deep negative forward book is reserves already spoken for.</p>
          </div>
