          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>External debt is what Indian residents owe to non-residents and must repay: government loans from the World Bank and other governments, companies' foreign loans and bonds, NRI deposits, trade credit. Short-term debt, due within a year, is what a run would hit first. In what currency the debt is owed we do not know from these tables: RBI's quarterly debt press release gives it, but DBIE does not carry it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {DEBT}</span>
            <p>External debt was <b id="ex-debt-total">–</b> in <span id="ex-debt-y">–</span>, against <b id="ex-debt-91">–</b> in <span id="ex-debt-91y">–</span>. It is <b id="ex-debt-gdp">–</b> of GDP; short-term debt is <b id="ex-debt-st">–</b> of the total; repaying and servicing it takes <b id="ex-debt-ds">–</b> of current receipts. The latest week's reserves equal <b id="ex-debt-cover">–</b> of that debt. NRI deposits of every maturity stood at <b id="ex-nri">–</b> in <span id="ex-nri-m">–</span>, and Indian exports invoiced in rupees reached <b id="ex-inv">–</b> of the total, fiscal year to <span id="ex-inv-m">–</span>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-sector" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Companies and banks, not the government, owe most of it</h3>
            <div class="sub">External debt by who borrowed, share of the total, {Q.sect}.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-sector">–</div><div class="c">the largest borrower</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-sector" style="position:relative"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, external debt of India, quarterly (DBIE report 660), borrower-by-maturity layout, shares computed by India Atlas from rupee values (the same shares in any currency at one date).</span>
