  <!-- § 12 misreadings: evergreen -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">12</span><h2>How to misread these numbers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li><span class="no">1</span><div><h3>"A trade deficit means India is losing to the world."</h3><p>India has bought more goods than it sold almost every year since independence, through its fastest growth. Services and remittances pay for most of it. The number to watch is the current account and how it is financed, not the goods gap alone.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">2</span><div><h3>"The reserves fell, so the RBI sold dollars."</h3><p>Not necessarily. Gold, euros and yen in the reserves are valued in dollars every week, so a stronger dollar or a fall in gold shrinks the pile with no selling at all. The RBI's own purchases and sales are published separately, a few weeks later.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">3</span><div><h3>"The rupee hit a record low, so Indian exports must be booming."</h3><p>Only if Indian prices did not rise as fast. The real effective exchange rate, which adjusts for prices across 40 trading partners, often moves far less than the dollar rate.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">4</span><div><h3>"Reserves are the government's savings."</h3><p>They are held by the RBI, and much of the pile is the counterpart of foreign money that came in and can leave. Spending them is defending the rupee, not funding roads.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">5</span><div><h3>"FDI hit a record, so foreign money is pouring in."</h3><p>Gross inflows are the headline; repatriation by foreign investors and Indian investment abroad are taken out before it reaches the balance of payments. Net direct investment can be small, even negative, in a year of record gross inflows.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">6</span><div><h3>"This quarter's deficit is the worst in years."</h3><p>Check which estimate. The first release of a quarter is often revised by billions; compare first estimates with first estimates, or wait for the revision.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">7</span><div><h3>"Remittances are what migrants earn and send home."</h3><p>The headline includes NRIs withdrawing money from deposits they already hold in India, and gifts and donations. Money sent home for families is the larger part, but not all of it.</p></div></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

